==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 04 NOTES — iptables Part 1
Date: 17-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- iptables is the real firewall engine; ufw (Linux journal Day 21) is a friendlier frontend to it
- The filter table has 3 built-in chains: INPUT (to this machine), OUTPUT (from it), FORWARD (through it)
- Rules are checked TOP TO BOTTOM, first match wins - order matters, a DROP above an ACCEPT wins
- -A appends to the bottom, -I inserts at a specific position (usually the top)
- DROP silently discards; REJECT discards AND tells the sender - genuinely different behavior
- -F flushes (deletes) all rules in a table - the fast way back to a clean, safe state
- Delete rules by line number from the BOTTOM up, since earlier numbers shift after each delete

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] iptables -L -v -n  /  -S  (viewing current rules)
[ ] iptables -t filter -L  vs  -t nat -L (different tables)
[ ] iptables -A INPUT ... -j ACCEPT / DROP
[ ] iptables -I INPUT 1 ... (inserting above an existing rule)
[ ] iptables -D INPUT <line>  and  iptables -F (cleanup)

paste or describe what you saw :
$ sudo iptables -L INPUT -v -n --line-numbers (after Part C)
> 

$ ping -c 3 <eth0-ip> (before and after the ACCEPT insert)
> 


